---
// src/pages/the-place.astro — The Place
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import PullQuote from '../components/PullQuote.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import ReviewNote from '../components/ReviewNote.astro';
import Reveal from '../components/Reveal.astro';
import CTASection from '../components/CTASection.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Place"
  description="Moravian Falls, Prayer Mountain, and the stewardship of the land. Ember Ridge stands within a wider spiritual story shaped by the Moravian witness and generations of people who gave themselves to God."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="The Place"
    title="A Land Marked by Prayer"
    lede="Ember Ridge will stand within a wider spiritual story — one shaped by the Moravian witness, the history of Prayer Mountain, and generations of people who gave themselves to God."
  />

  <Section labelledby="moravian-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <Reveal>
        <SectionHeading
          id="moravian-heading"
          eyebrow="Herrnhut to North Carolina"
          title="A historic pattern that resonates with this calling"
        />
      </Reveal>
      <div class="mt-12">
        <Prose>
          <p>
            In 1727, the community at Herrnhut entered a season of spiritual renewal
            and established a continual prayer watch that endured for more than one
            hundred years. Their worship and intercession helped fuel one of the most
            influential missionary movements in modern Church history. When Moravians
            later established communities in North Carolina, they carried a disciplined
            life of prayer, work, worship, stewardship, education, and mission into the
            region.
          </p>
          <p>
            Moravian Falls and Prayer Mountain have since become gathering places for
            believers drawn to prayer, prophetic ministry, healing, and encounter with
            God. Many have testified to an unusual clarity in prayer on the mountain.
          </p>
        </Prose>

        <Callout title="How we hold these testimonies">
          <p>
            Firebrand Revivalists receives those testimonies with gratitude while
            keeping Scripture, humility, and wise discernment at the center. The
            purpose is not to romanticize a location, but to steward responsibly a
            place where generations have sought the Lord.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="charcoal" labelledby="witness-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <Reveal>
        <SectionHeading
          id="witness-heading"
          eyebrow="A Wider Testimony"
          title="When people make room for God over time, a culture can form"
        />
      </Reveal>
      <div class="mt-12">
        <Prose>
          <p>
            The testimony associated with Frances Metcalfe, the Golden Candlestick
            fellowship, and the women remembered as the Ladies of Gold offers another
            meaningful point of reference. Their story centers on extended worship,
            intercession, consecration, spiritual discernment, and dependence upon the
            Holy Spirit. They gathered faithfully over many years until the presence of
            God became the defining reality of their fellowship.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ember Ridge does not seek to reproduce their ministry, but it recognizes a
            shared principle: when people make room for God over time, a culture can
            form in which prayer, obedience, supernatural faith, and practical
            provision become woven together.
          </p>
          <p>
            A 1997 prophetic word concerning Prayer Mountain also emphasized praise,
            prayer, intercession, truth, healing, restored relationships, and the need
            to hear and obey God. Firebrand views that word as one strand within the
            spiritual testimony of the area — to be weighed alongside Scripture, mature
            counsel, the fruit of prior stewardship, and the confirmations the family
            has received over many years.
          </p>
        </Prose>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section labelledby="baton-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <Reveal>
        <SectionHeading
          id="baton-heading"
          eyebrow="Stewardship of the Land"
          title="Received as a baton handed forward"
        />
      </Reveal>
      <div class="mt-12">
        <Prose>
          <p>
            The current lodge has already been used by ministry leaders who welcomed
            people from around the world. Ember Ridge will honor that history rather
            than erase it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the property is entrusted to Firebrand Revivalists, the transition will
            be received as a baton handed forward. What has been built in prior seasons
            will be respected, while the property is renewed for the particular
            assignment now being formed: a house that ministers to the Lord, receives
            leaders and families, reforms unhealthy patterns, restores strength, and
            sends people back into the work of the Kingdom.
          </p>
        </Prose>

        <ReviewNote label="Needs review">
          The lodge property has not yet been acquired. All references to the property
          describe a vision under discernment and due diligence, not a completed
          purchase. Confirm the current status of negotiations before publishing, and
          avoid naming the seller or exact address until closing.
        </ReviewNote>

        <PullQuote cite="Psalm 127:1">
          Unless the Lord builds the house, those who build it labor in vain.
        </PullQuote>

        <Prose>
          <p class="pt-2">
            <a href="/remodel-and-grounds" class="btn-quiet">See how the campus is envisioned →</a>
          </p>
        </Prose>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <CTASection />
</BaseLayout>
